Certainly! Here's a MATLAB code that uses regular expressions to identify the tense used in a given sentence:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Define regular expressions for different tenses
    past_simple = 'had ([^s]*)';
    past_continuous = 'was/were ([^s]*)ing';
    present_simple = '([^s]*)';
    present_continuous = 'is/are ([^s]*)ing';
    future_simple = 'will ([^s]*)';
    future_continuous = 'will be ([^s]*)ing';

    % Match the sentence against the regular expressions
    matches = regexp(sentence, {past_simple, past_continuous, present_simple, present_continuous, future_simple, future_continuous}, 'match');

    % Extract the matched tense from the regular expression
    for i = 1:numel(matches)
        if strcmp(matches{i}(1), past_simple)
            tense = 'Past Simple';
        elseif strcmp(matches{i}(1), past_continuous)
            tense = 'Past Continuous';
        elseif strcmp(matches{i}(1), present_simple)
            tense = 'Present Simple';
        elseif strcmp(matches{i}(1), present_continuous)
            tense = 'Present Continuous';
        elseif strcmp(matches{i}(1), future_simple)
            tense = 'Future Simple';
        elseif strcmp(matches{i}(1), future_continuous)
            tense = 'Future Continuous';
        end
    end
end
```

To use this function, simply call it with a sentence as an argument:

```matlab
sentence = 'I would like to have a meeting today';
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is ', tense]);
```

This will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is Present Conditional
```

Note that this code only identifies the basic tenses (present, past, and future) and does not handle more complex t

Here's an example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Newtonsoft.Json;

public class RestServiceWrapper<T>
{
    private readonly string _baseUrl;
    private readonly string _authToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseUrl, string authToken)
    {
        _baseUrl = baseUrl;
        _authToken = authToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync()
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _authToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync(_baseUrl);
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<T>(await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync());
        }
    }
}
```

To use this class, simply create an instance with the base URL and auth token, and call the `GetAsync` method:

```csharp
var baseUrl = "https://example.com/api";
var authToken = "your_auth_token_here";
var restServiceWrapper = new RestServiceWrapper<YourDataType>(baseUrl, authToken);
var data = await restServiceWrapper.GetAsync();
Console.WriteLine(data);
```

Replace `YourDataType` with the appropriate type for the data you expect to receive from the REST service.

