The given MATLAB code calculates the distance between two points on the Earth's surface, taking into account its ellipsoidal shape. Here's how you can use it to find the distance between San Francisco and London:

```matlab
% Set the values for the Earth's semi-major axis (a), semi-minor axis (b),
% and the flattening (f)
a = 6378137; % meters
b = 6356752.3142; % meters
f = (a-b)/a;

% Convert the latitudes and longitudes from degrees to radians
lat1 = deg2rad(37.7749);
lon1 = deg2rad(-122.4194);
lat2 = deg2rad(51.5074);
lon2 = deg2rad(-0.1278);

% Calculate the radius of curvature in the prime vertical at each point
N1 = a/sqrt(1-f*sin(lat1)^2);
N2 = a/sqrt(1-f*sin(lat2)^2);

% Calculate the Cartesian coordinates of each point
x1 = (N1+h1)*cos(lat1)*cos(lon1);
y1 = (N1+h1)*cos(lat1)*sin(lon1);
z1 = ((1-e2)*N1+h1)*sin(lat1);
x2 = (N2+h2)*cos(lat2)*cos(lon2);
y2 = (N2+h2)*cos(lat2)*sin(lon2);
z2 = ((1-e2)*N2+h2)*sin(lat2);

% Calculate the distance between the two points
d = sqrt((x1-x2)^2 + (y1-y2)^2 + (z1-z2)^2);

% Display the result
fprintf('The distance between San Francisco and London is %.2f meters.\n', d);
```

